/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:478:In many scenarios when one applies approximate decomposition results, as for example Theorem~\ref{thm:quasirandom}, it is important that the graph $G-\phi(\cH)$ has `small' maximum degree.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:480:{We remark that set and vertex testers in our main result are very flexible and capture many desirable properties.} For example, Theorem~\ref{thm:quasirandom} implies the approximate decomposition result in~\cite{ABCT:19} when restricted to graphs of bounded maximum degree.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:483:In this paper, we give one example how to apply Theorem~\ref{thm:quasirandom}. 
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:484:By exploiting set testers, we can combine it with Keevash's results on hypergraph decompositions to decompose pseudorandom graphs into regular spanning graphs
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:487:For this result, we need a stronger notation of pseudorandomness as used by Keevash in~\cite{keevash:18b}.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:498:Theorem~\ref{thm:application} makes progress on a conjecture by Glock, Joos, Kim, K\"uhn and Osthus who conjecture in~\cite{GJKKO:18} that $K_n$ can be decomposed into any collection $\cH$ of regular bounded degree graphs with $\sum_{H\in \cH}e(H)=\binom{n}{2}$.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:508:They prove that such graphs $\widetilde{H}$ can be embedded into~$G$ by a probabilistic algorithm in a very uniform way.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:509:For some $\gamma \ll \alpha$, they apply this algorithm to $\gamma n$ graphs in $\widetilde{\cH}$ in turn.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:511:Nevertheless, after embedding $\gamma n$ graphs, they remove all `used' edges from $G$ and repeat.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:512:At the end, they eliminate all edge overlaps by unembedding several vertices and complete the packing by {utilising} a thin edge slice put aside at the beginning.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:516:and which is consistent with our partial packing so far.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:517:{Our `Approximate Packing Lemma', stated in Section~\ref{sec:approx packing}, performs one such step using} an auxiliary hypergraph where we aim to find a large matching which is pseudorandom with respect to certain weight functions.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:518:At the end, we complete the packing by also using a thin edge slice similar to~\cite{KKOT:19}.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:525:We hope that our alternative proof of the blow-up lemma for approximate decompositions paths the way for further developments in the field.
/tmp/tmp.1TfCAgTpt3/approximate_decomp_blow_up.tex:527:Some ideas in this paper are taken from our recent article with Glock~\cite{EGJ:19b} on rainbow embeddings in graphs.
