-# Verified generator for arXiv:2001.03506
+# Perfect-matching packings for arXiv:2001.03506
 
-Status: the module and every local gate pass, but the required external oracle run is not yet valid. OpenRouter reports `limit_remaining: 0` with no reset date, and `harden.py` received only HTTP 403 “key limit exceeded” responses. It correctly refused to count those as model failures. The three transcripts are infrastructure evidence, not a hardness verdict.
-
-## Profile
+> **Status:** the generator and every local gate pass, but the required Step 4
+> oracle claim is incomplete. OpenRouter rejected the bare, structural-hint, and
+> placebo runs before inference with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`.
+> Those service errors are preserved in the harness-owned transcripts and are not
+> counted as model failures.
 
-| field | value |
+| Profile field | Value |
 |---|---|
-| `TRACK` | B — efficient mechanical solver disclosed |
-| native domain | combinatorics |
-| object regime | finite discrete |
-| computational core | exact cover / edge-disjoint bipartite matchings |
-| certificate form | integer tuple, arranged as explicit vertex-map arrays |
-| intended intuition | symmetry: simultaneous host permutations carry an edge around a perfect-matching orbit |
-| domain essentiality | native |
-| reduction | none |
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain / object regime | combinatorics / finite discrete |
+| Computational core | permutation (bipartite edge factorization) |
+| Certificate | matrix certificate: one permutation row per guest |
+| Native objects | regular bipartite host, perfect-matching guests, packing maps |
+| Intended intuition | change of variables: reciprocal coordinates reveal translation factors |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
 
 ## Problem and trust model
 
-The source is Ehard and Joos, [*A short proof of the blow-up lemma for approximate decompositions*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.03506). Section 1.1 defines a packing as vertex maps that are injective on each input graph and injectively map all source edges to host edges. Section 4.1 gives the cluster-level conflict-free form, and Step 1 of Section 4.2 identifies those maps with a matching in an auxiliary hypergraph.
+Ehard and Joos's [*A short proof of the blow-up lemma for approximate
+decompositions*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.03506) defines a packing in Section
+1.1 as injective guest-vertex maps whose guest edges map injectively into one
+host. This module uses that definition directly in its two-cluster special case.
+Every guest is a spanning perfect matching. Its left side is normalized to the
+equally labelled host vertices, so a witness is a matrix of right-side image
+permutations. `verify` checks the row shapes, permutations, host-edge membership,
+and global edge-disjointness exactly; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.
 
